      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.602,
            0.042,
            0.848,
            0.058
          ],
          "content": "Atomic Foundations of Matter"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "N 163"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.088,
            0.53,
            0.125
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 9.1: Let us investigate a physical change"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.128,
            0.572,
            0.163
          ],
          "content": "1. Place a clean and dry 100 mL beaker on a digital weighing balance."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.165,
            0.572,
            0.2
          ],
          "content": "2. Set the balance reading to zero by pressing the tare or reset button."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.203,
            0.535,
            0.221
          ],
          "content": "3. Pour about 50 mL of water into the beaker."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.224,
            0.572,
            0.258
          ],
          "content": "4. Add a spatula full of common salt to the water contained in the beaker."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.261,
            0.572,
            0.296
          ],
          "content": "5. **Record** the reading on the weighing balance (Fig. 9.1a)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.298,
            0.572,
            0.334
          ],
          "content": "6. Swirl until the added salt dissolves and record your observations (Fig. 9.1b)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.336,
            0.574,
            0.489
          ],
          "content": "What do you **observe**? You may **notice** that the mass of the solution is equal to the sum of the masses of water and salt taken. This shows that there is practically no change in the mass during the formation of a solution, which is a physical change. This is true for all physical changes. You can repeat the above activity by weighing a piece of paper before and after tearing it into pieces, and observe whether its mass changes or not."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.491,
            0.572,
            0.528
          ],
          "content": "Now, let us find out whether this is true for chemical changes as well."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.592,
            0.08,
            0.952,
            0.258
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.591,
            0.303,
            0.947,
            0.502
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.585,
                0.27,
                0.819,
                0.3
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 9.1: (a) Weight of water and undissolved salt"
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.588,
                0.507,
                0.826,
                0.536
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 9.1: (b) Weight of solution of salt and water"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.534,
            0.572,
            0.572
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 9.2: Let us investigate a chemical change"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.574,
            0.775,
            0.643
          ],
          "content": "You have learnt about various chemical changes. Do you remember what happened when baking soda was added to vinegar? A gas, carbon dioxide, was formed during this chemical change and the reaction is represented as —"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.779,
            0.578,
            0.853,
            0.615
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.646,
            0.893,
            0.664
          ],
          "content": "**Vinegar + Baking soda (Sodium hydrogencarbonate) Carbon dioxide + Other substances**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.666,
            0.563,
            0.702
          ],
          "content": "Let us explore whether the mass remains the same before and after the change."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.709,
            0.343,
            0.728
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Experimental set-up 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.73,
            0.562,
            0.766
          ],
          "content": "1. Place a clean, dry 100 mL conical flask and a medium-sized balloon on a weighing balance."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.768,
            0.562,
            0.803
          ],
          "content": "2. Set the balance reading to zero by pressing the tare or reset button."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.806,
            0.562,
            0.84
          ],
          "content": "3. Pour about 20 mL of vinegar or lemon juice into the conical flask."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.843,
            0.563,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": "4. Take about 2 g of baking soda (sodium hydrogencarbonate) and put it into the balloon."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.88,
            0.562,
            0.933
          ],
          "content": "5. Keep the balloon filled with baking soda on the weighing balance next to the conical flask. Record the initial reading (Fig. 9.2a)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.583,
